  "theory": {
    "intro": "Integrating an exponential undoes differentiating it. Since differentiating $e^{ax+b}$ multiplies by $a$, integrating it divides by $a$: $\\int e^{ax+b}\\,dx = \\dfrac{1}{a}e^{ax+b} + C$. The index itself never changes.",
    "facts": [
      "$\\int e^{x}\\,dx = e^{x} + C$, because $e^{x}$ is its own derivative.",
      "$\\int e^{ax+b}\\,dx = \\dfrac{1}{a}e^{ax+b} + C$ for constants $a \\ne 0$ and $b$.",
      "Differentiating multiplies by $a$, so integrating divides by $a$; losing that $\\dfrac{1}{a}$ is the most common error.",
      "A constant factor passes straight through: $\\int ke^{ax+b}\\,dx = \\dfrac{k}{a}e^{ax+b} + C$.",
      "When $a$ is negative the divisor is negative, so $\\int e^{-x}\\,dx = -e^{-x} + C$.",
      "The index is never raised by one, because the power rule does not apply to $e^{x}$.",
      "Rewrite $\\dfrac{1}{e^{ax+b}}$ as $e^{-ax-b}$ before integrating.",
      "There is no product rule for integration, so expand a product such as $e^{x}\\left(e^{x}+1\\right)$ first.",
      "For a definite integral write $\\left[F(x)\\right]_{p}^{q}$ and compute $F(q) - F(p)$ in that order.",
      "Check any primitive by differentiating it: the result must be the original integrand."
    ],
    "steps": [
      "Rewrite the integrand so every term is a single power of $e$, expanding brackets or splitting fractions first.",
      "Write down $e$ to the same index, unchanged.",
      "Divide by $a$, the coefficient of $x$ in the index, keeping any constant factor.",
      "Add $+C$ for an indefinite integral, or evaluate $\\left[F(x)\\right]_{p}^{q}$ for a definite one."
    ]
  },
